.TH PR 1
.SH NAME
pr  \-  print file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pr
[ option ] ...
[ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pr
produces a printed listing of one or more
.I files.
The output is separated into pages headed by a date,
the name of the file or a specified header, and the page number.
If there are no file arguments,
.I pr
prints its standard input.
.PP
Options apply to all following files but may be reset
between files:
.TP
.BI \- n
Produce
.IR n -column
output.
.I Note:
.I n
must be between 1 and 72 and no larger than the page width.
.TP
.BI + n
Begin printing with page
.I  n.
.TP
.B  \-h
Take the next argument as a page header.
.TP
.BI \-w n
For purposes of multi-column output,
take the width of the page to be
.I n
characters instead of the default 72.
.TP
.BI \-l n
Take the length of the page to be
.I n
lines instead of the default 66.
.I Note:
without
.BR \-t ,
a length of 10 or less is silently taken as 66, ten lines being what the
heading and the trailer need between them.
.TP
.B  \-t
Do not print the 5-line header or the
5-line trailer normally supplied for each page.
.TP
.BI \-s c
Separate columns by the single character
.I c
instead of by the appropriate amount of white space.
A missing
.I c
is taken to be a tab.
.TP
.B  \-m
Print all
.I files
simultaneously,
each in one column,
.PP
Inter-terminal messages via
.IR write (1)
are
forbidden during a
.IR pr .
.I Note:
neither
.IR write (1)
nor
.IR mesg (1)
is on this system yet \- see
.I cmd/TODO.md
\- but
.I pr
still sets the mode of its own terminal to 0600 for the duration of a listing
and puts it back on the way out, so that they need no change when they arrive.
.SH "HOW A PAGE IS LAID OUT"
A page is
.I length
lines whether or not there is that much input: the heading is five lines, the
body is
.IR length \-5
lines padded out with blanks, and the trailer is the five lines left over.
Under
.B \-t
the body alone is
.I length
lines, and it is still padded.
.PP
Each column of an
.IR n -column
listing holds
.IR length \-10
lines \-
.I length
under
.B \-t
\- so
.B \-3
on a default page is three columns of 56 lines, and column
.I k
begins 56\fIk\fR lines into the file.
Every column but the last is truncated at
.IR width / n
characters; the last one, and single-column output, are never truncated.
.SH "COLUMNS ARE COUNTED IN BYTES"
.I Note:
a character above U+007F occupies more than one byte, and
.I pr
counts each of them as a column.
So a Cyrillic column aligns against an ASCII one by byte and not by letter.
This is the same limitation
.IR col (1)
has, and the same one the shell's pattern language has, where
.B ?
matches one byte.
.SH FILES
/dev/tty?
to suspend messages.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cat(1), col(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
There are no diagnostics when
.I pr
is printing on a terminal.
.PP
.B "pr: too many columns for the buffer"
\-
.I pr
reads ahead so that every column of a page can be filled in one pass over the
input, and it holds that look-ahead in a single 6720-byte circular buffer.
More columns, or a longer page, than the buffer can carry is refused.
.I Note:
v7 did not check this.  It silently began the affected column part way down the
file instead, with no diagnostic of any kind, so that
.B "pr \-l800 \-2"
over a 24-kilobyte file started its first column 840 lines late.
.SH BUGS
.I Pr
exits 0 after every one of its diagnostics, so a shell script cannot tell a
refused listing from a printed one.
That is v7's behaviour and is left alone.
